1
00:00:19,490 --> 00:00:26,260
 It's in the line, next tape, and replay, after yes, it's, uh, no, it's Johnson.

2
00:00:26,540 --> 00:00:30,920
 Johnson wins, and it's a world record.

3
00:00:34,480 --> 00:00:37,500
 ...of one second.

4
00:00:38,270 --> 00:00:40,520
 Realize that's 20 times less than one second.

5
00:00:40,640 --> 00:00:41,700
 It takes your breath away.

6
00:00:41,980 --> 00:00:43,000
 And what precision.

7
00:00:43,430 --> 00:00:44,830
 What mastery of time.

8
00:00:45,630 --> 00:00:47,690
 Uh, mastery of time, what tempo.

9
00:00:48,440 --> 00:00:51,040
 Always in a big hurry, never a second to spare.

10
00:00:51,440 --> 00:00:52,520
 Our modern time.

11
00:00:52,980 --> 00:00:54,080
 You think about it, though.

12
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:55,660
 One five hundredths of a second?

13
00:00:56,670 --> 00:00:58,960
 The others, how come they're late?

14
00:00:59,320 --> 00:01:00,680
 No, they've lost track of the time.

15
00:01:01,040 --> 00:01:03,540
 Now is a good time to tell you a really fascinating story.

16
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:05,940
 All about the invention of time, in fact.

17
00:01:06,320 --> 00:01:07,670
 We know all about it.

18
00:01:08,410 --> 00:01:09,490
 Yeah, don't waste our time.

19
00:01:09,770 --> 00:01:12,270
 Oh, invented, maestro.

20
00:01:12,490 --> 00:01:13,970
 I thought the time always existed.

21
00:01:14,270 --> 00:01:15,550
 Oh, yes, it's always existed.

22
00:01:15,790 --> 00:01:17,510
 There's never been a time without time.

23
00:01:17,890 --> 00:01:21,610
 But we had to learn how to measure it, cut it up, and count the pieces.

24
00:01:21,610 --> 00:01:26,410
 We divided it into, look here, into the distant past, thousands of years ago.

25
00:01:26,790 --> 00:01:28,080
 I feel hungry.

26
00:01:28,710 --> 00:01:29,850
 Is that time to eat?

27
00:01:30,160 --> 00:01:33,070
 You're right, I'm hungry too! It's time to eat!

28
00:01:33,070 --> 00:01:46,910
 Yes, already it's time.

29
00:01:46,910 --> 00:01:48,640
 You're leaving?

30
00:01:48,640 --> 00:01:59,760
 When the shadow is right here, otherwise I'll be worried.

31
00:01:59,760 --> 00:02:10,880
 It was like being out of time.

32
00:02:10,880 --> 00:02:15,330
 When we dream, that's when time plays its tricks.

33
00:02:15,330 --> 00:02:18,560
 Did you know that for a long time, time wasn't measured?

34
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:31,390
 You take ancient Egypt...

35
00:02:31,390 --> 00:02:59,100
 Sun goddess Bean reborn!

36
00:02:59,100 --> 00:03:06,750
 ...or maybe it was the Babylonians, thought up the idea of the weak.

37
00:03:06,750 --> 00:03:08,550
 Babylonians?

38
00:03:08,550 --> 00:03:12,010
 They lived in the place that we call today, Iraq.

39
00:03:12,010 --> 00:03:17,020
 They built a very tall tower, the Tower of Babel, to observe the sky.

40
00:03:17,020 --> 00:03:28,020
 See the full moon appear every 30 days.

41
00:03:28,020 --> 00:03:30,270
 We might divide it into four weeks of seven days.

42
00:03:30,270 --> 00:03:32,910
 Four times seven, that makes 28.

43
00:03:32,910 --> 00:03:36,030
 There are two days missing, perhaps eight days in every week.

44
00:03:36,030 --> 00:03:38,430
 That's two days too many in every week.

45
00:03:38,430 --> 00:03:39,070
 Won't do.

46
00:03:39,070 --> 00:03:41,890
 A seven and a half day week.

47
00:03:41,890 --> 00:03:43,890
 No, it wouldn't do.

48
00:03:43,890 --> 00:03:46,800
 Some weeks will begin during the day and others at night.

49
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:48,200
 Let's say seven days.

50
00:03:48,200 --> 00:03:49,600
 No, I say eight days.

51
00:03:49,600 --> 00:03:50,800
 I said mine first.

52
00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:52,360
 Well, that doesn't count.

53
00:03:52,360 --> 00:03:54,690
 We'll roll and see who we've done.

54
00:03:54,690 --> 00:04:12,520
 Well, crow war means bad luck.

55
00:04:12,520 --> 00:04:17,080
 No, children, somehow I don't believe that story is true to you.

56
00:04:17,080 --> 00:04:22,460
 The truth is, nobody knows why or how the seven-day week came about.

57
00:04:22,460 --> 00:04:28,790
 Certain peoples decided a week had five days, others decided six days, and sometimes even ten days.

58
00:04:28,790 --> 00:04:33,600
 That's right, man has sought to, well, cut up time.

59
00:04:33,600 --> 00:04:35,870
 The big problem is the night.

60
00:04:35,870 --> 00:04:47,590
 Sush, work's too slow. It's over two years, and my pyramid still isn't ready.

61
00:04:47,590 --> 00:04:50,970
 At this rate, I will be dead before the pyramid is even built.

62
00:04:50,970 --> 00:04:57,060
 The slaves work from sunup to sunset each day, O'Pharaoh. I don't have enough men.

63
00:04:57,060 --> 00:05:00,960
 There are no more workers. You make them work after sunset.

64
00:05:00,960 --> 00:05:08,540
 I can count only the hours of sunlight. The workers have their roles. They will want overtime. We could go over a budget.

65
00:05:08,540 --> 00:05:12,550
 Of course we can! We can count the hours at night.

66
00:05:12,550 --> 00:05:13,890
 Behold O'Pharaoh!

67
00:05:13,890 --> 00:05:16,730
 See how the water flows out at a constant rate.

68
00:05:16,730 --> 00:05:20,930
 I have marked the level of water each hour during the day, and they're the same at night.

69
00:05:20,930 --> 00:05:25,120
 We can measure time even when there is no sun.

70
00:05:25,120 --> 00:05:29,300
 You work till the third level, the third hour of the night.

71
00:05:29,300 --> 00:05:32,200
 And you will all receive extra wheat and garlic.

72
00:05:32,200 --> 00:05:32,700
 Hmm.

73
00:05:32,700 --> 00:05:34,740
 All right, we'll try it.

74
00:05:34,740 --> 00:05:41,050
 Just another three hours and we get what's promised.

75
00:05:41,050 --> 00:05:43,530
 Overtime, overtime, overtime!

76
00:05:43,530 --> 00:05:53,150
 It's time!

77
00:05:53,150 --> 00:06:35,630
 the mediterranean in greece public clocks kept time day and night counting off the hours usual

78
00:06:35,630 --> 00:06:40,350
 the politicians are cheating the anger of the gods will soon strike down upon us and what's more

79
00:06:40,350 --> 00:06:46,370
 citizens of athens your taxes that's windbag demetrius he never stops talking and every time

80
00:06:46,370 --> 00:06:52,840
 he talks it's the same we needed a way to limit him and so we use a small clepsydra there it is

81
00:06:52,840 --> 00:06:59,550
 oh i say to you who are these politicians fellow citizens the gods will take vengeance believe me

82
00:06:59,550 --> 00:07:04,250
 It's always the same. Lucky the water's almost all run out.

83
00:07:04,250 --> 00:07:09,410
 Soon your taxes will exceed your income. The politicians will want more! More water!

84
00:07:09,410 --> 00:07:23,030
 Eclepsedras are being invented all the time. There's the one that I mentioned,

85
00:07:23,030 --> 00:07:29,480
 and another one by Hero of Alexandria. In Rome, at the same time, there were even pocket clocks.

86
00:07:29,480 --> 00:07:32,610
 They're not terribly accurate, it's true.

87
00:07:32,610 --> 00:07:48,080
 Here for half a division. You're wrong. I'm even a little early.

88
00:07:48,080 --> 00:07:50,630
 Look at my pocket clock. I tell you, you're late!

89
00:07:50,630 --> 00:07:53,830
 Sorry, old friend, I'm not. I told you I'm even early.

90
00:07:54,160 --> 00:07:55,860
 Then your pocket clock's no good.

91
00:07:56,610 --> 00:07:59,900
 The sun is at the zenith citizens, it is midday.

92
00:08:01,390 --> 00:08:05,810
 But the hours of darkness were still a problem. They were erratic.

93
00:08:05,990 --> 00:08:06,830
 Why was that?

94
00:08:07,010 --> 00:08:09,810
 Because the night had 12 hours, same as the day.

95
00:08:10,240 --> 00:08:12,360
 However, in winter, the nights are longer.

96
00:08:12,750 --> 00:08:16,130
 And so those hours were longer in winter and shorter in summer.

97
00:08:16,480 --> 00:08:19,550
 The shorter the hours, the better I like it, because it's less work.

98
00:08:19,550 --> 00:08:23,180
 Then we'll all give you an hourglass with sand.

99
00:08:23,320 --> 00:08:25,880
 Of course it could measure only short periods of time.

100
00:08:26,330 --> 00:08:28,250
 But as always, there were exceptions.

101
00:08:29,190 --> 00:08:31,550
 For instance, the one Charlemagne had.

102
00:08:32,570 --> 00:08:36,500
 It was turned over once every 12 hours.

103
00:08:37,760 --> 00:08:43,810
 There wasn't a moment to lose.

104
00:08:43,810 --> 00:08:56,510
 The Byzantine Empire decided to honor the Emperor and present him with a magnificent gift.

105
00:09:05,970 --> 00:09:09,090
 Came the oil clock. It was, alas, not terribly accurate.

106
00:09:09,790 --> 00:09:12,530
 As for the candle clock, what did they say?

107
00:09:12,550 --> 00:09:15,070
 It was invented by a Saxon king, Alfred the Great.

108
00:09:15,760 --> 00:09:17,700
 Oh Lord, restore my kingdom.

109
00:09:18,060 --> 00:09:21,120
 and I swear I will consecrate a third of my time to prayer.

110
00:09:22,280 --> 00:09:32,640
 Get thy sacred oath, Magister.

111
00:09:33,230 --> 00:09:35,810
 Candles will be lit one after another in secret.

112
00:09:36,560 --> 00:09:38,880
 Know, sir, each candle burns four hours,

113
00:09:39,120 --> 00:09:42,980
 therefore you must pray for as long as two candles burn one after the other.

114
00:09:59,240 --> 00:10:06,900
 Your Majesty.

115
00:10:07,280 --> 00:10:10,960
 The Chinese, who were never a people to do things in the simplest way,

116
00:10:11,320 --> 00:10:14,010
 came up with an improvement, an enormous water clock,

117
00:10:14,010 --> 00:10:21,090
 putting in motion the wheel which is linked to the drum.

118
00:10:21,830 --> 00:10:23,610
 It is here that one reads the time.

119
00:10:23,940 --> 00:10:27,020
 Hear the phases of the moon, as well as the movement of the planet.

120
00:10:28,440 --> 00:10:32,420
 Keep it secret.

121
00:10:38,160 --> 00:10:41,860
 So song for the Emperor, your enemy.

122
00:10:42,850 --> 00:10:47,090
 When the sun will rise, movements of the planet, phases of the moon, and it tells time.

123
00:10:47,620 --> 00:10:49,340
 You make an excellent copy.

124
00:10:50,150 --> 00:10:50,970
 You want to see it?

125
00:10:52,210 --> 00:10:55,610
 Water from here flows down into the basin.

126
00:10:55,870 --> 00:10:58,750
 Once it's full, the basin drives the wheel you see here,

127
00:10:58,990 --> 00:11:01,420
 which is linked to the drum which you see here.

128
00:11:01,420 --> 00:11:04,260
 The drum, you see, drives the what you call it.

129
00:11:04,260 --> 00:11:05,380
 What I want to see is this thing working.

130
00:11:05,770 --> 00:11:06,770
 Let it in the water.

131
00:11:21,700 --> 00:11:23,540
 Suffer yourself how well it works.

132
00:11:24,660 --> 00:11:32,320
 I'd like to know just how that story ends.

133
00:11:33,780 --> 00:11:38,140
 Just a few years later, unfortunately, the Song Dynasty was overthrown and replaced,

134
00:11:38,260 --> 00:11:41,940
 and the remarkable clock was dismantled and piece by piece taken to Peking.

135
00:11:42,760 --> 00:11:44,510
 Wow, Celestial Emperor.

136
00:11:45,010 --> 00:11:48,710
 Your clock is assembled in working order as you desire.

137
00:11:50,640 --> 00:11:54,980
 Oh, most honored son of heaven, this clock is bound to bring evil.

138
00:11:55,470 --> 00:11:58,330
 Recall, Celestial One, it comes from the last dynasty.

139
00:11:58,470 --> 00:11:59,390
 We shall destroy it.

140
00:11:59,390 --> 00:12:01,450
 What is your opinion, Salmi Chu?

141
00:12:02,580 --> 00:12:06,620
 This clock is truly admirable. Not one like it in the whole world.

142
00:12:06,620 --> 00:12:12,480
 I'd consider it, oh, son of heaven, a tragic loss beyond repair for humanity if it were destroyed.

143
00:12:12,480 --> 00:12:17,150
 This object is harmful, and besides, it houses evil spirits. I can show you proof.

144
00:12:17,150 --> 00:12:25,290
 And so it was that Sousong's clock, one of the most magnificent creations of all time, was destroyed.

145
00:12:25,290 --> 00:12:29,450
 So you see, children, man's stupidity extends to all lands.

146
00:12:29,450 --> 00:12:31,710
 But now, let's go back in time.

147
00:12:31,710 --> 00:12:36,130
 It's in France that the very first mechanical clock was to be invented.

148
00:12:36,130 --> 00:12:38,730
 the ancestor of all of today's clocks and watches.

149
00:12:39,050 --> 00:12:41,150
 And it was in a Benedictine abbey.

150
00:12:47,280 --> 00:12:55,850
 Once again, we're late for morning prayers, my brothers.

151
00:12:56,070 --> 00:12:57,030
 What's the matter?

152
00:12:57,570 --> 00:12:59,620
 I don't know, Brother Gilbert.

153
00:13:02,050 --> 00:13:05,830
 And again, and so the water clock stopped.

154
00:13:06,120 --> 00:13:08,640
 Oh, Lord, once again deprived of our wine.

155
00:13:09,030 --> 00:13:10,700
 You've been in the world, my brother.

156
00:13:10,870 --> 00:13:12,200
 Find us some solution.

157
00:13:12,510 --> 00:13:14,980
 As it happens, I'm working on a clock now.

158
00:13:14,980 --> 00:13:17,380
 You see, gravity pulls down this heavy weight,

159
00:13:17,500 --> 00:13:19,880
 pulling on the rope, which makes the cylinder turn.

160
00:13:20,240 --> 00:13:22,620
 The cog wheel is then also turned.

161
00:13:23,050 --> 00:13:26,870
 At the moment designated, the mallet will strike this little bell right here.

162
00:13:27,080 --> 00:13:29,050
 You really think it'll work, my brother?

163
00:13:29,760 --> 00:13:31,390
 Hmm, if God so wills.

164
00:13:34,850 --> 00:13:41,510
 Of course, brother. Gilbert's clock.

165
00:13:41,800 --> 00:13:42,530
 You know it.

166
00:14:02,010 --> 00:14:08,140
 Bravo, my brother.

167
00:14:08,480 --> 00:14:10,480
 It is the Lord's will, my brother.

168
00:14:10,480 --> 00:14:13,780
 If clocks began by waking bell ringers,

169
00:14:14,180 --> 00:14:17,760
 they soon began to ring bells themselves, morning, noon, and night.

170
00:14:19,030 --> 00:14:23,810
 One of the most famous bell tower clocks is in the cathedral at Strasbourg in France.

171
00:14:37,770 --> 00:14:52,380
 They're marked out on the face in a circle, going around from noon to midnight and midnight to noon.

172
00:14:52,720 --> 00:14:56,120
 It's quite simple, yes, but it took a very long time to perfect it.

173
00:14:56,930 --> 00:15:00,630
 This hour dial, children, we owe it, as it happens, to the Italians.

174
00:15:01,290 --> 00:15:05,530
 Some of their clockmakers created extraordinary pieces in Padua in the 14th century.

175
00:15:06,040 --> 00:15:11,670
 Here, look, I just invented a dial that you can see at a distance, and it's easy to read the hours.

176
00:15:11,670 --> 00:15:14,910
 A dial that's round, what absolute nonsense, absolutely useless.

177
00:15:15,230 --> 00:15:19,390
 But the earth is also round, besides the sun that determines the hours of the day.

178
00:15:19,490 --> 00:15:21,190
 It, too, traces a path that's circular.

179
00:15:21,470 --> 00:15:23,170
 But clocks don't run in circles.

180
00:15:23,950 --> 00:15:25,290
 We'll see about that.

181
00:15:30,730 --> 00:15:36,450
 Excellent. I congratulate you. It is splendid.

182
00:15:37,030 --> 00:15:39,400
 I can glance at it and tell the time.

183
00:15:40,060 --> 00:15:43,340
 Twelve hours of the day followed by the hours of night.

184
00:15:43,700 --> 00:15:45,580
 We can divide the hours into minutes.

185
00:15:45,900 --> 00:15:48,180
 Who is it who invented this superb object?

186
00:15:48,540 --> 00:15:50,360
 It's me, Jacopo Dondi.

187
00:15:50,360 --> 00:15:53,040
 My son, Giovanni, was a great help.

188
00:15:53,220 --> 00:15:56,280
 Jacopo Dondi, from now on, you bear the title of clockmaker.

189
00:15:56,930 --> 00:16:00,440
 For our university, would you like to make a quite special clock?

190
00:16:00,500 --> 00:16:00,800
 Special?

191
00:16:01,100 --> 00:16:04,040
 Oh, yes. I mean, a clock that will not only tell the time,

192
00:16:04,340 --> 00:16:06,560
 it must also give the phases of the moon,

193
00:16:06,920 --> 00:16:09,180
 the movements of the planets, and also...

194
00:16:09,180 --> 00:16:11,910
 Can you make such a clock?

195
00:16:12,110 --> 00:16:13,430
 It won't be easy.

196
00:16:13,590 --> 00:16:14,230
 Well, can you?

197
00:16:15,370 --> 00:16:17,330
 Me, I can do it. I'm your man.

198
00:16:17,410 --> 00:16:19,790
 I'll make a clock to do everything you said. You'll see.

199
00:16:20,090 --> 00:16:21,750
 I will do it, Professor.

200
00:16:22,050 --> 00:16:24,370
 Only it will take some time. A great deal of time.

201
00:16:24,730 --> 00:16:25,750
 Me too, I'll do it.

202
00:16:25,920 --> 00:16:26,830
 Good luck to you.

203
00:16:43,470 --> 00:17:08,320
 Let's consider one thing.

204
00:17:08,380 --> 00:17:10,360
 One fact stands out, and it's the most...

205
00:17:10,360 --> 00:17:11,490
 17 years of work.

206
00:17:12,140 --> 00:17:14,170
 What a shame your father's not here to say it.

207
00:17:16,430 --> 00:17:20,460
 Dear Giovanni, if your clock can really do everything I asked,

208
00:17:20,770 --> 00:17:22,510
 then, my lad, you have my word.

209
00:17:23,050 --> 00:17:25,070
 Your name will go down in history forever.

210
00:17:25,510 --> 00:17:27,520
 I can only say I did the best I could.

211
00:17:28,900 --> 00:17:30,030
 What a machine!

212
00:17:30,990 --> 00:17:33,420
 I made you a nice clock.

213
00:17:33,420 --> 00:17:36,540
 It's marvelous, and it will tell you more than you can want to know.

214
00:17:36,760 --> 00:17:38,140
 You'll see. You will see.

215
00:17:38,400 --> 00:17:48,200
 To make a comparison,

216
00:17:48,980 --> 00:17:50,630
 are six sides.

217
00:17:50,630 --> 00:17:53,920
 Here you can read the hours as well as the minutes.

218
00:17:53,920 --> 00:17:57,560
 - On mine as well, here's hours and minutes here.

219
00:17:57,560 --> 00:18:01,330
 - Here you can read the month and the day all year long.

220
00:18:01,330 --> 00:18:03,420
 - Two here, you see?

221
00:18:03,420 --> 00:18:05,100
 - Here are shown faces of the moon,

222
00:18:05,100 --> 00:18:06,840
 the times of sunrise and sunset,

223
00:18:06,840 --> 00:18:08,700
 and the position of the sun in the sky.

224
00:18:08,700 --> 00:18:11,220
 - No, me too, me too, me too, me too.

225
00:18:11,220 --> 00:18:15,310
 - It can predict the eclipses, both lunar and solar.

226
00:18:15,310 --> 00:18:18,310
 As you can see, every feature is clearly displayed

227
00:18:18,310 --> 00:18:19,730
 on the several faces.

228
00:18:19,730 --> 00:18:22,700
 And finally, the movement annually of the planets

229
00:18:22,700 --> 00:18:28,780
 the solar system. The whole machine operates on this weight. All you have to do is lift it up once

230
00:18:28,780 --> 00:18:35,990
 every day. Mine too. Curious, isn't it? Your clock is nearly an exact copy of mine. I must say,

231
00:18:35,990 --> 00:18:41,740
 that's a coincidence. Yes, curious indeed. Extraordinary that here two men working on

232
00:18:41,740 --> 00:18:47,010
 their own over a period of 17 years managed to produce identical masterpieces. Well, it proves

233
00:18:47,010 --> 00:18:53,020
 what a great clockmaker I am. The only thing is, with my astrarium, there's a small clicking noise

234
00:18:53,020 --> 00:18:54,760
 when the cogwheels turn.

235
00:18:56,730 --> 00:18:58,010
 Whereas your machine, Guido,

236
00:18:58,130 --> 00:19:00,210
 is absolutely silent when it runs.

237
00:19:00,490 --> 00:19:01,050
 Quite so.

238
00:19:01,130 --> 00:19:03,070
 My machine here makes no noise at all.

239
00:19:03,150 --> 00:19:03,450
 None.

240
00:19:04,450 --> 00:19:06,040
 God bless you.

241
00:19:06,480 --> 00:19:08,060
 It wasn't me who sneezed.

242
00:19:08,100 --> 00:19:08,640
 It's me.

243
00:19:10,640 --> 00:19:11,340
 It's me.

244
00:19:11,440 --> 00:19:12,840
 I catch colds all the time.

245
00:19:13,240 --> 00:19:14,140
 Yes, all the time.

246
00:19:14,660 --> 00:19:15,140
 Achoo.

247
00:19:15,560 --> 00:19:16,070
 Achoo.

248
00:19:18,040 --> 00:19:19,470
 Well, well.

249
00:19:19,550 --> 00:19:21,050
 Your mechanisms caught your cold.

250
00:19:21,560 --> 00:19:22,340
 No, no.

251
00:19:22,620 --> 00:19:23,680
 You haven't the right.

252
00:19:23,900 --> 00:19:24,720
 It's my secret.

253
00:19:24,720 --> 00:19:25,540
 My machine.

254
00:19:25,680 --> 00:19:26,160
 My property.

255
00:19:26,340 --> 00:19:27,420
 My magical clock.

256
00:19:27,660 --> 00:19:38,780
 Hello, I'm only here in case it breaks down. The mechanism's fragile. Mary.

257
00:19:44,790 --> 00:19:52,400
 Dial clocks were installed in towns and villages, and important men couldn't do without them.

258
00:19:52,700 --> 00:19:55,140
 You take Louis XI, King of France.

259
00:19:56,370 --> 00:20:00,100
 You haven't forgotten my little traveling clock, I hope.

260
00:20:00,370 --> 00:20:01,900
 No, sire, it's right there.

261
00:20:03,190 --> 00:20:05,750
 What is the exact time?

262
00:20:06,810 --> 00:20:08,430
 Ten in the morning, sire.

263
00:20:16,010 --> 00:20:21,570
 Stream on!

264
00:20:21,570 --> 00:20:45,440
 What is the time?

265
00:20:57,000 --> 00:20:57,470
 Four, near.

266
00:21:00,490 --> 00:21:03,080
 This here stick, it just hits my little finger.

267
00:21:03,280 --> 00:21:04,380
 It couldn't hardly be mistaken.

268
00:21:06,170 --> 00:21:09,450
 It's four in the afternoon, your majesty.

269
00:21:10,970 --> 00:21:12,680
 That's right.

270
00:21:12,880 --> 00:21:14,560
 Despite centuries of progress,

271
00:21:14,860 --> 00:21:17,340
 many people still used the old sundial,

272
00:21:17,750 --> 00:21:20,840
 the Greek gnomon, over 2,000 years old.

273
00:21:21,460 --> 00:21:24,220
 You just mark your hand and read the stick's shadow,

274
00:21:24,440 --> 00:21:27,420
 but everything was to change with the pendulum.

275
00:21:27,420 --> 00:21:29,070
 Oh, yes, the pendulum.

276
00:21:29,290 --> 00:21:30,490
 I remember Galileo.

277
00:21:34,220 --> 00:21:39,400
 Right, Galileo discovered that the time for each swing

278
00:21:39,400 --> 00:21:41,080
 depends on the length of the pendulum,

279
00:21:41,260 --> 00:21:47,420
 So it became the precision timer for clockmakers, and watches too, naturally.

280
00:21:47,420 --> 00:21:54,750
 They really needed accuracy, certainly in the Navy.

281
00:21:54,750 --> 00:21:57,760
 Navigation was a very imprecise science.

282
00:21:57,760 --> 00:22:26,220
 Came Galileo's pendulum clock and his telescope.

283
00:22:26,220 --> 00:22:38,900
 See Jupiter's satellites?

284
00:22:38,900 --> 00:22:43,790
 As well as you just have to watch them.

285
00:22:43,790 --> 00:22:45,940
 Watching, I'm watching.

286
00:22:45,940 --> 00:22:47,970
 It's easy to say, sir.

287
00:22:47,970 --> 00:22:52,760
 An error of five minutes means a two-degree error in longitude.

288
00:22:52,760 --> 00:23:00,560
 No real effort was made until a naval tragedy occurred off the English coast on the rocks of the Silly Islands.

289
00:23:00,560 --> 00:23:09,440
 Muralty would award a prize of 20,000 pounds to anyone who found a way of accurately determining longitude.

290
00:23:09,440 --> 00:23:13,720
 Robert Hooke had already invented the spring-driven clock.

291
00:23:13,720 --> 00:23:19,130
 And when Hugens announced his spring-driven clock, Hooke would claim that his invention was stolen.

292
00:23:19,130 --> 00:23:22,400
 Clément invents the anchor escapement, and based on their work,

293
00:23:22,850 --> 00:23:25,670
 emerges the chronometer invented by John Harrison,

294
00:23:25,830 --> 00:23:29,550
 the so-called number four, accurate enough to win the Admiralty Prize.

295
00:23:30,210 --> 00:23:32,160
 Well, it wasn't long before the modern clock,

296
00:23:32,370 --> 00:23:35,110
 and the first wristwatches appeared in 1910.

297
00:23:35,560 --> 00:23:36,160
 Oh, yes.

298
00:23:36,520 --> 00:23:39,740
 There was extraordinary progress leading to the atomic clock,

299
00:23:39,820 --> 00:23:42,880
 which is accurate to within one second every 3,000 years,

300
00:23:43,020 --> 00:23:45,020
 and the quartz watch with its tiny battery.

301
00:23:45,620 --> 00:23:46,460
 You mean like mine?

302
00:23:46,460 --> 00:23:48,860
 Oh, like mine, like mine, like mine, like mine.

303
00:23:48,920 --> 00:23:51,620
 So men, in a way, has mastered time.

304
00:23:51,620 --> 00:23:55,520
 He's divided it into minutes and then into seconds and then into hundreds of seconds

305
00:23:55,520 --> 00:23:59,160
 and time sets the pace of our civilization.

306
00:23:59,160 --> 00:24:04,570
 But have we really dominated time or have we truly become its slave?

